    # ========== PRACTICAL TECH (20) ==========
    {"instruction": "How do I create a chatbot?", "response": "Creating a chatbot:\n1. Define purpose — what will it do?\n2. Choose platform: WhatsApp, Telegram, website\n3. Simple approach: Use a backend function that receives messages and returns responses\n4. AI approach: Use an LLM API (like OpenRouter with free models) to generate responses\n5. HARZ approach: Backend function on Base44 + AI model = chatbot\n6. Test thoroughly before going live\n7. Monitor conversations and improve\nHARZ has 80+ AI agents built this way — AISHA for support, HAUWA for marketing, etc."},
    {"instruction": "What is Python used for in business?", "response": "Python in business:\n1. Automating repetitive tasks (reports, emails, data entry)\n2. Data analysis (sales, customers, trends)\n3. Web scraping (competitor prices, market research)\n4. Building APIs and web backends\n5. Creating tools and calculators\n6. Processing files (Excel, PDF, CSV)\n7. Machine learning (predictions, recommendations)\n8. Chatbots and AI assistants\nOur Python Programming book (N2,500) teaches all this with a real Sales Report Generator project."},
    {"instruction": "How do I automate my business tasks?", "response": "Automating business tasks:\n1. Identify repetitive tasks — emails, reports, reminders\n2. Use automation tools: Zapier, Make.com, IFTTT\n3. HARZ approach: Base44 automations (scheduled + entity-triggered)\n4. Python scripts for data processing\n5. WhatsApp auto-replies for customer FAQs\n6. Scheduled social media posts (Buffer, Later)\n7. Email autoresponders\n8. Payment confirmation webhooks\nHARZ has 45+ automations running: daily reports, CRM, product uploads, keep-alive, health checks."},
    {"instruction": "What is Node.js?", "response": "Node.js is a JavaScript runtime that lets you run JavaScript on servers (not just browsers):\n1. Used for building APIs, web servers, real-time apps\n2. Fast and efficient (non-blocking I/O)\n3. Huge ecosystem of packages (npm)\n4. Same language for frontend and backend (JavaScript)\n5. Used by: Netflix, Uber, LinkedIn, PayPal\n6. Frameworks: Express, Fastify, NestJS\nHARZ Cloud backend on Render uses Node.js — 163 routes, serving all 63+ platforms."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between SQL and NoSQL?", "response": "SQL vs NoSQL:\n\nSQL (relational):\n- Tables with rows and columns\n- Strict schema (must define structure)\n- MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite\n- Good for: transactions, complex queries, financial data\n\nNoSQL (non-relational):\n- Documents (like JSON), flexible schema\n- MongoDB, Firebase, DynamoDB\n- Good for: rapid development, varied data, scaling\n\nHARZ uses MongoDB (via Base44) — flexible for 200+ different entity types. Each entity has its own schema."},
    {"instruction": "What is Docker?", "response": "Docker is a tool for packaging applications with all their dependencies:\n1. Like a shipping container — everything inside works anywhere\n2. Solves 'it works on my machine' problem\n3. Package your app + all dependencies in a container\n4. Run the same container on any server\n5. Lightweight (uses less resources than VMs)\n6. Used by most modern companies\nFor HARZ: We use Render which handles deployment without needing Docker directly. But understanding it helps for advanced deployments."},
    {"instruction": "What is a REST API?", "response": "REST API is a way to build web APIs using HTTP methods:\n1. GET — retrieve data (like fetching products)\n2. POST — create new data (like placing an order)\n3. PUT/PATCH — update data\n4. DELETE — remove data\n\nExample: GET https://harz-cloud-backend.onrender.com/health returns system status\n\nREST is the most common API style. Simple, stateless, uses standard HTTP. All HARZ backend functions follow REST principles."},
    {"instruction": "How do I handle errors in programming?", "response": "Handling errors in programming:\n1. Try-catch blocks — catch errors before they crash\n2. Validate input — check data before processing\n3. Log errors — record what went wrong\n4. User-friendly messages — don't show raw errors to users\n5. Graceful degradation — app keeps working even if something fails\n6. Retry logic — try again if temporary failure\n7. Fallbacks — alternative if main path fails\n8. Monitor errors in production\nExample: If payment fails, show 'Payment failed, please try again' not 'Error: TypeError undefined'."},
    {"instruction": "What is authentication vs authorization?", "response": "Authentication vs Authorization:\n\nAuthentication (who are you?):\n- Login with email/password\n- Biometrics (fingerprint, face)\n- 2FA codes\n- Token verification\n\nAuthorization (what can you do?):\n- Role-based access (admin, user, guest)\n- Permission checks (can you edit this?)\n- Resource access (can you see this data?)\n\nHARZ has both: SSO for authentication (login once, access all 23 platforms) and RBAC for authorization (6 roles: Owner, Admin, Manager, User, Agent, Guest)."},
    {"instruction": "What is a database index?", "response": "A database index is like a book index — helps find data faster:\n1. Without index: scan every row (slow for large tables)\n2. With index: jump to the right rows (fast)\n3. Create indexes on columns you search/filter often\n4. Trade-off: faster reads, slower writes, more storage\n5. Don't over-index — each index has a cost\nExample: If you search products by category often, index the category column.\nHARZ uses Base44 which handles indexing automatically for entity queries."},
    {"instruction": "What is CI/CD?", "response": "CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment):\n\nCI: Every time you push code, it automatically tests:\n1. Run tests\n2. Check code quality\n3. Build the project\n4. Fail if errors found\n\nCD: If tests pass, automatically deploy:\n1. Push to production\n2. No manual intervention\n3. Rollback if something breaks\n\nHARZ uses a simple version: GitHub auto-deploys to Render on every push to main branch. GitHub Pages auto-deploys on every push."},
    {"instruction": "What is microservices architecture?", "response": "Microservices architecture splits an app into small, independent services:\n1. Each service handles one feature (auth, payments, products)\n2. Services communicate via APIs\n3. Each can be deployed independently\n4. Easier to scale and maintain\n5. If one fails, others keep working\n\nvs Monolith (everything in one app):\n- Simpler to start\n- Harder to scale\n\nHARZ uses a hybrid: Core backend on Render (monolith) + Base44 functions (microservices) + Cloudflare Workers (edge)."},
    {"instruction": "What is JWT?", "response": "JWT (JSON Web Token) is a secure way to handle authentication:\n1. User logs in → server creates a JWT\n2. JWT contains: user ID, role, expiry (encoded)\n3. Client sends JWT with every request\n4. Server verifies JWT → grants access\n5. No need to store session on server\n6. Works great for APIs and mobile apps\n7. Stateless — scales easily\nHARZ SSO uses JWT tokens — login once, use token across all 23 platforms."},
    {"instruction": "What is WebSocket?", "response": "WebSocket is a communication protocol for real-time data:\n1. Normal HTTP: client requests, server responds, connection closes\n2. WebSocket: connection stays open, both sides can send anytime\n3. Used for: chat apps, live notifications, real-time updates, games\n4. Faster than polling (constantly asking for updates)\nHARZ Agent Server uses WebSocket for real-time AI chat — messages appear instantly without page refresh."},
    {"instruction": "What is a CDN?", "response": "CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a network of servers worldwide:\n1. Copies your content to servers near users\n2. User in Nigeria gets content from nearby server (faster)\n3. Reduces load on your main server\n4. Handles traffic spikes better\n5. Cloudflare (free), AWS CloudFront, jsDelivr\nHARZ uses GitHub Pages (which has built-in CDN) and Cloudflare for additional speed. All our websites load fast even in Nigeria."},
    {"instruction": "What is serverless computing?", "response": "Serverless computing means you run code without managing servers:\n1. Write function → deploy → it runs when called\n2. No server management, no scaling worries\n3. Pay only for execution time\n4. Auto-scales to zero when not used\nExamples: AWS Lambda, Cloudflare Workers, Base44 functions\nHARZ uses Base44 backend functions — serverless, no server management. Also Cloudflare Workers for edge computing."},
    {"instruction": "What is version control?", "response": "Version control tracks changes to your code:\n1. Every change is recorded (who, what, when)\n2. Go back to any previous version\n3. Work on features in branches without affecting main\n4. Merge changes when ready\n5. Resolve conflicts when multiple people edit\nGit is the most popular version control system. GitHub is the most popular hosting platform. HARZ has 60+ repos on GitHub."},
    {"instruction": "What is the cloud?", "response": "The cloud means using remote servers over the internet instead of your own hardware:\n1. Storage — save files on remote servers (Google Drive, Dropbox)\n2. Computing — run apps on remote servers (Render, AWS)\n3. Database — store data on remote servers (MongoDB Atlas)\n4. Benefits: no hardware cost, scale easily, access from anywhere\n5. Pay-as-you-go (or free tiers)\nHARZ is 100% cloud: GitHub Pages (hosting), Render (backend), Base44 (database), Cloudflare (edge). Total cost: N0."},
    {"instruction": "What is responsive design?", "response": "Responsive design makes websites adapt to all screen sizes:\n1. Mobile (320-480px) — most Nigerian users\n2. Tablet (768-1024px)\n3. Desktop (1024px+)\nTechniques: CSS Media Queries, Flexbox, Grid, mobile-first\nHARZ websites are responsive — optimized for Infinix Hot 10i and similar devices. We keep pages under 50KB for fast loading on slow networks."},
    {"instruction": "What is an API key?", "response": "An API key is like a password for accessing an API:\n1. Identifies who you are\n2. Controls what you can access\n3. Tracks usage for billing\n4. Must be kept secret — never put in frontend code\n5. Regenerate if compromised\nHARZ keeps all API keys server-side (in Base44 functions or .env). Frontend never sees them. This is a security best practice."},
    {"instruction": "What is encryption?", "response": "Encryption scrambles data so only authorized people can read it:\n1. Plain text → encrypted (unreadable)\n2. Only with the right key can decrypt\n3. HTTPS = encrypted web traffic\n4. End-to-end encryption = only sender and receiver can read\n5. WhatsApp messages are end-to-end encrypted\nTypes: symmetric (same key), asymmetric (public/private key pair)\nHARZ uses HTTPS everywhere, encrypts sensitive data, and never stores passwords in plain text."},

    # ========== HEALTH DEEP DIVE (20) ==========
    {"instruction": "What are the symptoms of typhoid fever?", "response": "Typhoid symptoms:\n1. Persistent fever (gradually increasing)\n2. Headache\n3. Abdominal pain\n4. Weakness and fatigue\n5. Loss of appetite\n6. Constipation or diarrhea\n7. Rose-colored spots on chest/abdomen\n8. Dry cough\nIf you suspect typhoid, do a Widal test or blood culture. Treatment requires antibiotics. Drink clean water, wash hands, and avoid contaminated food.\nNote: I'm an AI. Always consult a healthcare professional."},
    {"instruction": "How can I treat common cold at home?", "response": "Home treatment for common cold:\n1. Rest — give your body time to fight\n2. Drink plenty fluids — water, warm tea, soup\n3. Steam inhalation — clears congestion\n4. Honey and ginger tea — soothes throat\n5. Salt water gargle — relieves sore throat\n6. Vitamin C — oranges, lemons\n7. Paracetamol for fever and pain\n8. Sleep with head elevated\n9. Avoid cold drinks\nMost colds resolve in 7-10 days. See a doctor if: high fever, difficulty breathing, symptoms worsen after 5 days."},
    {"instruction": "What causes stomach ulcer?", "response": "Stomach ulcer causes:\n1. H. pylori bacteria (most common)\n2. Long-term use of NSAIDs (ibuprofen, aspirin)\n3. Excessive alcohol\n4. Smoking\n5. Stress (worsens but doesn't cause)\n6. Spicy food (worsens but doesn't cause)\n\nSymptoms: burning stomach pain, bloating, nausea, loss of appetite\n\nPrevention: eat on time, avoid too many painkillers, limit alcohol, don't smoke, manage stress.\nTreatment requires medical diagnosis — see a doctor for proper medication."},
    {"instruction": "How can I prevent cholera?", "response": "Cholera prevention:\n1. Drink clean, safe water — boil or filter\n2. Wash hands with soap before eating and after toilet\n3. Cook food thoroughly\n4. Wash fruits and vegetables with clean water\n5. Avoid raw seafood\n6. Use proper toilet facilities\n7. Keep food covered\n8. Avoid street food prepared in unhygienic conditions\n9. Get vaccinated if available\nCholera causes severe watery diarrhea and dehydration. If you suspect cholera, go to hospital immediately — it can be fatal if untreated."},
    {"instruction": "What are the signs of pregnancy?", "response": "Early pregnancy signs:\n1. Missed period\n2. Nausea (morning sickness)\n3. Breast tenderness\n4. Fatigue\n5. Frequent urination\n6. Food cravings or aversions\n7. Mood changes\n8. Light spotting (implantation bleeding)\n9. Heightened sense of smell\n10. Headaches\n\nConfirm with a pregnancy test (available at pharmacies). If positive, start ante-natal care immediately.\nNote: I'm an AI. Consult a healthcare professional for medical advice."},
    {"instruction": "How can I care for a newborn baby?", "response": "Newborn care basics:\n1. Breastfeed exclusively for 6 months\n2. Keep baby clean — bath daily with warm water\n3. Dress baby appropriately — not too hot or cold\n4. Ensure immunizations on schedule\n5. Support baby's head and neck\n6. Put baby to sleep on back (prevents SIDS)\n7. Wash hands before handling baby\n8. Keep baby away from sick people\n9. Burp baby after feeding\n10. Change diapers frequently\n11. Watch for fever — see doctor if above 38°C\n12. Attend all post-natal checkups"},
    {"instruction": "What is the best diet for a diabetic person?", "response": "Diet for diabetes management:\n1. Eat complex carbs — brown rice, whole wheat, oats\n2. Avoid refined sugar — soda, candy, pastries\n3. Eat more vegetables — ugu, spinach, okra\n4. Lean protein — fish, chicken, beans, eggs\n5. Limit white rice and bread\n6. Eat smaller portions more frequently\n7. Avoid fried foods\n8. Drink water, not sugary drinks\n9. Limit fruit juices (eat whole fruits instead)\n10. Monitor blood sugar regularly\nNigerian tips: Replace white rice with beans or unripe plantain. Eat moi-moi instead of cake."},
    {"instruction": "How can I manage back pain?", "response": "Managing back pain:\n1. Maintain good posture — sit and stand straight\n2. Exercise — strengthen core muscles\n3. Avoid heavy lifting — use legs, not back\n4. Sleep on a firm mattress\n5. Apply heat or cold packs\n6. Stretch daily\n7. Maintain healthy weight\n8. Avoid sitting too long — take breaks\n9. Use proper chair with back support\n10. If severe or persistent, see a doctor\nCommon causes: poor posture, muscle strain, heavy lifting, long sitting. Most back pain improves with exercise and good posture."},
    {"instruction": "What are the benefits of drinking water?", "response": "Benefits of drinking water:\n1. Flushes toxins from body\n2. Improves digestion\n3. Boosts energy and brain function\n4. Supports kidney function\n5. Maintains skin health\n6. Regulates body temperature\n7. Aids weight loss (reduces hunger)\n8. Prevents headaches\n9. Supports immune system\n10. Improves physical performance\n11. Reduces risk of kidney stones\n12. Maintains blood pressure\nDrink 2-3 liters daily. In hot Nigerian weather, drink more. Carry a water bottle everywhere."},
    {"instruction": "How can I improve my eyesight?", "response": "Eye health tips:\n1. Eat vitamin A foods — carrots, sweet potatoes, eggs\n2. Wear sunglasses in bright sunlight\n3. Take screen breaks (20-20-20 rule: every 20 min, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds)\n4. Don't read in dim light\n5. Get enough sleep\n6. Exercise improves blood flow to eyes\n7. Quit smoking\n8. Get regular eye checkups\n9. Wear protective eyewear when needed\n10. Stay hydrated\nIf you have vision problems, see an optometrist. Don't self-medicate with eye drops."},
    {"instruction": "What is the importance of exercise for mental health?", "response": "Exercise and mental health:\n1. Releases endorphins — natural mood boosters\n2. Reduces anxiety and depression\n3. Improves sleep quality\n4. Boosts self-confidence\n5. Reduces stress hormones (cortisol)\n6. Improves focus and concentration\n7. Provides sense of achievement\n8. Social interaction (group exercise)\n9. Better body image\n10. Increases energy levels\nEven 15 minutes of walking daily makes a difference. HARZ MindCare platform tracks mental wellness alongside physical activity."},
    {"instruction": "How can I stop snoring?", "response": "Reducing snoring:\n1. Sleep on your side (not back)\n2. Lose excess weight\n3. Avoid alcohol before bed\n4. Treat nasal congestion\n5. Use extra pillow (elevate head)\n6. Stay hydrated\n7. Avoid smoking\n8. Exercise regularly\n9. Establish regular sleep schedule\n10. Consider anti-snoring devices\nIf snoring is severe with breathing pauses (sleep apnea), see a doctor — it can be dangerous."},
    {"instruction": "What foods should I avoid during pregnancy?", "response": "Foods to avoid during pregnancy:\n1. Raw or undercooked meat/fish\n2. Unpasteurized milk and cheese\n3. Raw eggs (including cookie dough)\n4. High-mercury fish (shark, swordfish)\n5. Excessive caffeine (limit to 1 cup)\n6. Alcohol (completely avoid)\n7. Processed meats (unless heated)\n8. Unwashed fruits/vegetables\n9. Excessive vitamin A (liver)\n10. Street food of questionable hygiene\n\nEat: fruits, vegetables, lean protein, whole grains, and take prenatal vitamins."},
    {"instruction": "How can I boost my metabolism?", "response": "Boosting metabolism naturally:\n1. Eat protein-rich foods — eggs, fish, beans\n2. Drink green tea\n3. Do strength training (muscle burns more calories)\n4. Drink cold water\n5. Get enough sleep\n6. Eat spicy foods (chili, pepper)\n7. Don't skip meals\n8. Drink coffee (in moderation)\n9. Stand up more (don't sit all day)\n10. Eat small frequent meals\nHigher metabolism = more calories burned = easier weight management. Exercise is the most effective metabolism booster."},
    {"instruction": "What are the symptoms of kidney disease?", "response": "Kidney disease symptoms:\n1. Changes in urination (more or less frequent)\n2. Foamy or dark urine\n3. Swelling in feet, ankles, hands, or face\n4. Persistent fatigue\n5. Shortness of breath\n6. Nausea and vomiting\n7. Loss of appetite\n8. Metallic taste in mouth\n9. Itchy skin\n10. High blood pressure\n\nRisk factors: diabetes, hypertension, family history, long-term painkiller use\nIf you have these symptoms, get a blood and urine test. Early detection prevents kidney failure."},
    {"instruction": "How can I take care of my teeth?", "response": "Dental care:\n1. Brush twice daily (morning and night)\n2. Use fluoride toothpaste\n3. Floss daily\n4. Avoid sugary drinks and snacks\n5. Visit dentist every 6 months\n6. Don't use teeth as tools (opening bottles)\n7. Limit coffee and tea (staining)\n8. Drink water after meals\n9. Replace toothbrush every 3 months\n10. Don't smoke (causes gum disease and oral cancer)\n11. Chew sugar-free gum after meals\nDental problems are expensive to fix — prevention is much cheaper. Brush properly: circular motions, 2 minutes."},
    {"instruction": "What is heat stroke and how do I prevent it?", "response": "Heat stroke (very dangerous in Nigerian heat):\nSymptoms: high body temperature (40°C+), confusion, hot dry skin, rapid pulse, headache, nausea, loss of consciousness\n\nPrevention:\n1. Drink plenty water (even when not thirsty)\n2. Avoid direct sun 11AM-3PM\n3. Wear light, loose clothing\n4. Use hat or umbrella\n5. Take breaks in shade\n6. Never leave children in cars\n\nIf heat stroke occurs:\n1. Move to cool area immediately\n2. Remove excess clothing\n3. Cool with water (not ice)\n4. Fan the person\n5. Seek medical help immediately\nHeat stroke can be fatal. Take Nigerian heat seriously."},
    {"instruction": "How can I manage my weight?", "response": "Weight management:\n1. Eat balanced meals — protein, vegetables, healthy carbs\n2. Portion control — use smaller plates\n3. Exercise regularly — 30 min daily\n4. Drink water before meals\n5. Limit processed and fried foods\n6. Reduce sugar intake\n7. Eat slowly — brain takes 20 min to feel full\n8. Get enough sleep\n9. Track what you eat\n10. Don't skip breakfast\n11. Limit eating after 8 PM\n12. Be consistent — it's a lifestyle, not a diet\nHealthy weight loss: 0.5-1 kg per week. Crash diets don't work long-term."},
    {"instruction": "What is the importance of vaccination?", "response": "Vaccination importance:\n1. Protects you from deadly diseases\n2. Protects others (herd immunity)\n3. Prevents outbreaks\n4. Saves money (treatment is expensive)\n5. Has saved millions of lives\n6. Safe and tested\n\nNigerian vaccination schedule includes: BCG, OPV, DPT, measles, yellow fever, hepatitis B, and others. Ensure children complete all vaccinations. Adults should get tetanus boosters and flu shots.\nVaccines are one of the greatest medical achievements. Don't believe misinformation — get vaccinated."},
    {"instruction": "How can I quit sugar addiction?", "response": "Quitting sugar:\n1. Gradual reduction — don't quit cold turkey\n2. Replace with fruits (natural sweetness)\n3. Read labels — sugar is hidden everywhere\n4. Avoid soda — drink water or zobo (no sugar)\n5. Cook at home (restaurant food has hidden sugar)\n6. Don't keep sweets at home\n7. Eat protein and fiber (keeps you full)\n8. Get enough sleep (cravings increase when tired)\n9. Manage stress (stress = sugar cravings)\n10. Allow occasional treats (don't be too strict)\nSugar addiction is real. It takes 2-3 weeks for cravings to decrease. Push through the first week."},

    # ========== FINANCE & MONEY (20) ==========
    {"instruction": "How do I open a Paystack account for my business?", "response": "To open Paystack:\n1. Go to paystack.com/signup\n2. Register with business email\n3. Fill business name and details\n4. Provide CAC number (if registered business)\n5. Add bank account for settlements\n6. Upload required documents (CAC, ID)\n7. Wait for approval (1-3 days for registered businesses)\n8. Get API keys (secret + public)\n9. Integrate on your website or use payment links\nFees: 1.5% local (cap N2,000), 3.9% international\nHARZ Paystack is LIVE — checkout links created for all 12 books at N3,200 each."},
    {"instruction": "What is compound interest and how does it work?", "response": "Compound interest is interest on your interest:\nSave N10,000/month at 10% annual return:\nYear 1: N126,000\nYear 5: N780,000\nYear 10: N2,000,000\nYear 20: N7,500,000\nYear 30: N22,000,000\nKey: Start early. Time is more powerful than the amount saved. Even N5,000/month compounds dramatically over decades. The earlier you start, the less you need to save overall."},
    {"instruction": "How can I save N100,000 in 6 months?", "response": "Saving N100,000 in 6 months (N16,667/month or N556/day):\n1. Set up automatic savings (auto-debit to savings account)\n2. Track every expense — cut unnecessary spending\n3. Reduce eating out — cook at home\n4. Use public transport instead of rides\n5. Cancel unused subscriptions\n6. Buy in bulk\n7. Sell things you don't need\n8. Side hustle — sell products, offer services\n9. Put savings where you can't easily access\n10. Stay disciplined — don't dip into savings\nUse PiggyVest, Cowrywise, or traditional ajo for automated savings."},
    {"instruction": "What is an emergency fund?", "response": "An emergency fund is savings for unexpected expenses:\n1. Target: 3-6 months of living expenses\n2. Keep in accessible but separate account\n3. Use ONLY for emergencies: job loss, illness, car repair\n4. Don't use for: vacations, gadgets, wants\n5. Start small — N50,000 first, build up\n6. Replenish after using\n7. Keep it liquid (not invested)\nAn emergency fund prevents debt when life happens. It's the foundation of financial security. Build it before investing."},
    {"instruction": "How do I track my expenses?", "response": "Tracking expenses:\n1. Write down every expense daily (notebook or app)\n2. Categorize: food, transport, bills, entertainment\n3. Use a simple Excel sheet\n4. Review weekly — spot patterns\n5. Budget for each category\n6. Track cash and digital payments\n7. Use apps: Spendee, Money Manager, or Excel\n8. Monthly summary: total spent vs budgeted\n9. Identify wasteful spending\n10. Adjust budget accordingly\nYou can't manage what you don't measure. Tracking is the first step to financial control."},
    {"instruction": "What are the best investment options in Nigeria?", "response": "Investment options in Nigeria:\n1. Treasury Bills — government-backed, safe, 10-15% return\n2. Mutual Funds — ARM, Stanbic, FBN (N5,000 minimum)\n3. Agriculture — farming (high returns, high risk)\n4. Real estate — land appreciates fast in Nigeria\n5. Stocks — Nigerian Stock Exchange (long-term)\n6. Crypto — Bitcoin, USDT (high risk, high reward)\n7. Business — invest in your own business (best ROI)\n8. Dollar savings — hedge against naira devaluation\nStart with: emergency fund → low-risk investments → higher risk as you grow. Never invest money you can't afford to lose."},
    {"instruction": "How do I create multiple income streams?", "response": "Creating multiple income streams:\n1. Primary: your main job or business\n2. Freelancing: Upwork, Fiverr (skills-based)\n3. Digital products: ebooks, courses (passive)\n4. Affiliate marketing: HARZ 10% commission\n5. Investments: treasury bills, mutual funds\n6. Rental income: rent out a room or property\n7. Content creation: YouTube, TikTok monetization\n8. Consulting: charge for your expertise\nDon't rely on one income source. Start with 2, build to 3-4. Each should require minimal ongoing time. Passive income is the goal."},
    {"instruction": "What is the 50/30/20 budgeting rule?", "response": "The 50/30/20 rule:\n50% — Needs: food, rent, transport, bills, essentials\n30% — Wants: entertainment, eating out, fashion, gadgets\n20% — Savings: emergency fund, investments, debt repayment\n\nExample with N100,000/month income:\nNeeds: N50,000\nWants: N30,000\nSavings: N20,000\n\nAdjust based on your situation. If income is low, increase needs % and reduce wants. As income grows, increase savings %. The key: always save something, even if small."},
    {"instruction": "How can I negotiate better prices?", "response": "Negotiating better prices:\n1. Do your research — know the market price\n2. Start lower than your target (leave room to go up)\n3. Be willing to walk away (your strongest tool)\n4. Buy in bulk for discounts\n5. Pay cash instead of transfer (some sellers prefer)\n6. Build relationship with sellers (regular customers get better deals)\n7. Compare prices from 3+ sellers\n8. Don't show too much interest (reduces your power)\n9. Ask 'is this your best price?'\n10. Time your purchase (end of month, holidays)\nIn Nigerian markets, negotiation is expected. Don't accept the first price — it's usually inflated."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between saving and investing?", "response": "Saving vs Investing:\n\nSaving:\n- Bank account, safe, liquid\n- Low return (2-5% per year)\n- For emergencies and short-term goals\n- Won't lose money\n- Inflation may eat the value\n\nInvesting:\n- Stocks, business, real estate, crypto\n- Higher return potential (10-50%+)\n- Higher risk (can lose money)\n- For long-term growth (3+ years)\n- Beats inflation over time\n\nBest approach: Save 3-6 months emergency fund first. Then invest the rest. Never invest your emergency fund."},
    {"instruction": "How do I avoid debt traps?", "response": "Avoiding debt traps:\n1. Don't borrow for consumption (clothes, phone, parties)\n2. Only borrow for investments that generate income\n3. Avoid loan apps with predatory rates (some charge 30%+ per month)\n4. Pay credit card balances in full each month\n5. Build emergency fund (prevents borrowing for emergencies)\n6. Live below your means\n7. Don't borrow to impress others\n8. If in debt, prioritize highest-interest debts first\n9. Negotiate with lenders if struggling\n10. Seek financial counseling if overwhelmed\nLoan apps in Nigeria can be dangerous — they access your contacts and shame you. Avoid them at all costs."},
    {"instruction": "What is financial freedom?", "response": "Financial freedom means your passive income covers your living expenses:\n1. You work because you want to, not because you have to\n2. Steps to freedom: earn more, spend less, save, invest, repeat\n3. Passive income sources: rental, dividends, digital products, business\n4. Track your 'freedom number': monthly expenses x 12 / expected return rate\n5. Example: N200,000/month expenses → N2.4M/year → at 10% return, need N24M invested\n6. Start small, be consistent, be patient\n7. Most people reach it in 10-20 years of disciplined saving and investing\nFinancial freedom is a marathon, not a sprint. Start today."},
    {"instruction": "How can I make money with my phone?", "response": "Making money with your phone in Nigeria:\n1. Sell products on WhatsApp/Instagram\n2. Freelance: write, design, manage social media\n3. Create content: TikTok, YouTube, Instagram\n4. Affiliate marketing: share HARZ links, earn 10%\n5. Online surveys (Swagbucks, though limited in Nigeria)\n6. Virtual assistant: manage emails, calendars\n7. Teach/tutor online\n8. Sell photos (Shutterstock)\n9. Play games (though earnings are small)\n10. App testing (UserTesting)\nYour phone is a business tool. The key is using it to create value, not just consume content."},
    {"instruction": "What is insurance and do I need it?", "response": "Insurance protects you from financial loss:\n1. Health insurance — covers medical bills\n2. Life insurance — supports family if you die\n3. Property insurance — covers damage to home/business\n4. Vehicle insurance — covers accidents (mandatory in Nigeria)\n5. Business insurance — covers business risks\n\nDo you need it? Yes, especially health and vehicle. Even basic insurance prevents one event from destroying your finances.\nIn Nigeria: NHIS for health (affordable), NAICOM for business. Start with health insurance — medical emergencies are the #1 cause of bankruptcy."},
    {"instruction": "How do I send money internationally from Nigeria?", "response": "Sending money internationally from Nigeria:\n1. Grey.co — virtual accounts, send/receive USD/GBP/EUR\n2. Payoneer — receive from platforms, send to bank\n3. Geegpay — virtual accounts, international transfers\n4. Crypto (USDT) — fastest, cheapest for large amounts\n5. WorldRemit — send to family abroad\n6. Sendcash — crypto-based international transfers\n7. Traditional banks — expensive, slow, limited\nFor receiving: Payoneer, Grey, Gumroad, Paddle. For sending: Crypto (USDT) is cheapest and fastest. Avoid traditional bank wire transfers — fees are high."},
    {"instruction": "What is a credit score and does it work in Nigeria?", "response": "Credit score in Nigeria:\n1. Measures your creditworthiness (how reliable you are at paying debts)\n2. Managed by Credit Bureaus: CRC, CreditRegistry, FirstCentral\n3. Built by: taking and repaying loans, paying bills on time\n4. Checked by: banks, lenders, some employers\n5. Good score = easier loan approval, lower interest rates\n6. Bad score = loan rejection, higher rates\n7. Check your score: CRC website or app\n\nTo build credit in Nigeria:\n- Pay loans on time\n- Pay utility bills regularly\n- Avoid multiple loan applications\n- Use credit cards responsibly (if available)"},
    {"instruction": "How do I do bookkeeping for my small business?", "response": "Simple bookkeeping for small business:\n1. Record every transaction (income and expense) daily\n2. Use a notebook or simple Excel sheet\n3. Columns: Date, Description, Income, Expense, Balance\n4. Keep all receipts\n5. Separate personal and business money\n6. Reconcile with bank statement monthly\n7. Calculate profit: Total Income - Total Expenses\n8. Review monthly: where is money coming from and going to?\n9. Save 20% of profit\n10. Use accounting apps as you grow: Wave (free), Zoho Books\nOur Excel Mastery book (N2,500) teaches how to build proper bookkeeping spreadsheets."},
    {"instruction": "What are treasury bills in Nigeria?", "response": "Treasury Bills (T-Bills) in Nigeria:\n1. Government-backed investment (very safe)\n2. Sold by CBN through banks\n3. Returns: 10-15% per year (better than savings)\n4. Minimum investment: N50,000 (varies by bank)\n5. Tenors: 91, 182, 364 days\n6. Interest paid upfront (you pay less than face value)\n7. Can be used as collateral for loans\n8. How to buy: Visit your bank, ask for T-bills form\n9. Good for: parking money safely while earning decent returns\n10. Risk: very low (government doesn't default)\nT-Bills are the safest investment in Nigeria for people who want guaranteed returns above inflation."},
    {"instruction": "How do I price my services competitively?", "response": "Pricing your services:\n1. Calculate your costs (time, materials, overhead)\n2. Research competitors' prices\n3. Add your desired profit margin\n4. Consider your experience level (beginners charge less)\n5. Value-based pricing: charge based on result for client\n6. Don't underprice (devalues your work)\n7. Don't overprice (lose clients)\n8. Offer packages (basic, standard, premium)\n9. Get 50% upfront payment\n10. Increase prices as you gain experience\nNigerian freelance rates: Writing N2,000-N10,000/article, Design N5,000-N50,000/project, Web dev N50,000-N500,000/project."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between revenue and profit?", "response": "Revenue vs Profit:\n\nRevenue (turnover): Total money from sales\nExample: You sell 100 books at N2,500 = N250,000 revenue\n\nProfit: What's left after costs\nCosts: N1,000/book (production) + N15,000 (marketing) = N115,000\nProfit = N250,000 - N115,000 = N135,000\n\nGross profit: Revenue - direct costs\nNet profit: Revenue - all costs (including taxes, rent, etc.)\n\nKey: Many businesses have high revenue but low or negative profit. Always track profit, not just revenue. Cash flow is even more important — profit on paper means nothing without cash."},

