    # ========== GENERAL KNOWLEDGE (20) ==========
    {"instruction": "What is the internet?", "response": "The internet is a global network of connected computers:\n1. Started in the 1960s as a military project (ARPANET)\n2. Now connects billions of devices worldwide\n3. Runs on protocols: TCP/IP, HTTP, DNS\n4. Key services: web (websites), email, file transfer, streaming\n5. No single owner — decentralized network\n6. ISPs (Internet Service Providers) provide access\n7. In Nigeria: MTN, Airtel, Glo, 9Mobile provide internet access\n8. Bandwidth and speed vary by location and provider\nThe internet has transformed how we communicate, learn, work, and do business. HARZ operates entirely on the internet — all 63+ platforms are web-based."},
    {"instruction": "What is 5G?", "response": "5G is the fifth generation of mobile internet:\n1. 100x faster than 4G\n2. Lower latency (almost instant response)\n3. Can connect more devices simultaneously\n4. Enables: self-driving cars, remote surgery, IoT, VR/AR\n5. Available in parts of Nigeria (MTN, Airtel)\n6. Requires 5G-compatible phone\n7. More expensive than 4G currently\n8. Will gradually expand across Nigeria\nFor most HARZ users, 4G is sufficient. Our platforms are optimized to work even on 3G connections."},
    {"instruction": "What is the metaverse?", "response": "The metaverse is a concept of a virtual world:\n1. Digital spaces where people interact as avatars\n2. Virtual and augmented reality combined\n3. Virtual real estate, events, shopping, work\n4. Companies like Meta (Facebook) investing billions\n5. Still in early stages — not mainstream yet\n6. Challenges: hardware, adoption, privacy\n7. Could transform: education, work, entertainment, commerce\nFor now, focus on practical digital skills. The metaverse may become relevant in 5-10 years."},
    {"instruction": "What is IoT (Internet of Things)?", "response": "IoT is everyday objects connected to the internet:\n1. Smart home — lights, thermostat, security cameras\n2. Wearables — fitness trackers, smartwatches\n3. Industrial — sensors in factories\n4. Agriculture — soil sensors, irrigation\n5. Smart cities — traffic, waste, energy management\n6. Examples: Alexa, smart TV, fitness band\nHARZ has an IoT entity system — IoTDevice, IoTAlert, IoTAutomation for tracking and managing smart devices in the ecosystem."},
    {"instruction": "What is big data?", "response": "Big data refers to extremely large datasets:\n1. Volume — massive amounts of data\n2. Velocity — data generated at high speed\n3. Variety — data in many formats (text, video, sensor)\n4. Veracity — data quality and accuracy\n5. Sources: social media, sensors, transactions, websites\n6. Analyzed with: Python, SQL, Hadoop, Spark\n7. Used for: predictions, recommendations, optimization\n8. Examples: Netflix recommendations, Google search, Amazon suggestions\nHARZ collects data from 63+ platforms — customer interactions, orders, CRM, analytics — forming our own big data ecosystem."},
    {"instruction": "What is the gig economy?", "response": "The gig economy is work based on short-term contracts:\n1. Freelancing — Upwork, Fiverr\n2. Ride-sharing — Uber, Bolt\n3. Delivery — food delivery apps\n4. Micro-tasks — data labeling, surveys\n5. Content creation — YouTube, TikTok\n6. Pros: flexibility, multiple income streams, be your own boss\n7. Cons: no benefits, unstable income, no job security\n8. Growing rapidly in Nigeria — especially freelancing and content creation\nHARZ supports the gig economy — our books teach skills for freelancing, and our platforms enable selling digital products independently."},
    {"instruction": "What is digital literacy?", "response": "Digital literacy is the ability to use technology effectively:\n1. Basic: using a computer, smartphone, internet\n2. Communication: email, messaging, video calls\n3. Information: searching, evaluating, using online information\n4. Creation: making documents, presentations, content\n5. Safety: protecting yourself online\n6. Problem-solving: using tech to solve problems\n7. Advanced: programming, data analysis, digital marketing\nDigital literacy is no longer optional — it's essential for every job and business. HARZ Digital Learning books are designed to build digital literacy from zero to professional."},
    {"instruction": "What is a startup?", "response": "A startup is a young company designed to grow fast:\n1. Usually tech-based or innovative\n2. Seeks to solve a problem in a new way\n3. Often funded by investors (angels, VCs)\n4. High risk, high potential reward\n5. Examples: Flutterwave, Paystack, Jumia (Nigerian startups)\n6. Key difference from small business: startups aim to scale rapidly\n7. Stages: idea → seed → series A/B/C → IPO or acquisition\n8. Most startups fail — but those that succeed can be huge\nHARZ is like a startup — started from zero, built 63+ platforms, aiming for massive scale across Nigeria and Africa."},
    {"instruction": "What is e-commerce?", "response": "E-commerce is buying and selling online:\n1. Types: B2C (business to consumer), B2B (business to business), C2C (consumer to consumer)\n2. Platforms: Jumia, Konga, Amazon, Shopify\n3. Digital products: ebooks, courses, software (highest margins)\n4. Physical products: require inventory, shipping\n5. Payment: Paystack, bank transfer, cards, crypto\n6. Key metrics: conversion rate, average order value, customer acquisition cost\n7. In Nigeria: WhatsApp is the #1 e-commerce platform\nHARZ does e-commerce through Gumroad, Getly, and HarzDM Marketplace — all digital products, instant delivery."},
    {"instruction": "What is cloud computing in simple terms?", "response": "Cloud computing in simple terms:\nImagine instead of buying your own computer, you rent one over the internet. That's cloud computing.\n\nInstead of:\n- Buying servers → Rent from AWS, Render\n- Storing files on hard drive → Store on Google Drive\n- Installing software → Use web apps\n\nBenefits: cheaper, scale easily, access from anywhere, no maintenance.\n\nHARZ is 100% cloud: GitHub Pages (websites), Render (backend), Base44 (database). We own zero physical servers. Total cost: N0/month."},
    {"instruction": "What is a SWOT analysis?", "response": "SWOT analysis helps you understand your business:\n\nS — Strengths (internal, positive): What you do well. HARZ: 63+ platforms, 300+ products, AI agents, zero infrastructure cost.\n\nW — Weaknesses (internal, negative): What to improve. HARZ: limited marketing budget, relies on one person, hardware constraints.\n\nO — Opportunities (external, positive): Market trends. HARZ: growing digital economy in Nigeria, increasing internet penetration, youth population.\n\nT — Threats (external, negative): Risks. HARZ: competition from bigger platforms, regulatory changes, economic instability.\n\nUse SWOT to make strategic decisions. Review quarterly."},
    {"instruction": "What is a competitive advantage?", "response": "Competitive advantage is what makes customers choose you over others:\n\nTypes:\n1. Price — cheapest option\n2. Quality — better product\n3. Service — better customer experience\n4. Speed — faster delivery\n5. Niche — serving a specific market others ignore\n6. Brand — reputation and recognition\n7. Network effects — more users = more value (like social media)\n8. Technology — better tools or methods\n\nHARZ competitive advantages:\n- Nigerian-focused (local context, Hausa/Pidgin support)\n- 63+ integrated platforms (one account, everything)\n- AI-powered (7 agents, 24/7 support)\n- Zero infrastructure cost (sustainable low pricing)\n- Affordable (N2,500-N6,000 products)"},
    {"instruction": "What is a business model?", "response": "A business model is how you make money:\n\nCommon models:\n1. Direct sales — sell products (HARZ books)\n2. Subscription — recurring monthly payment\n3. Marketplace — connect buyers and sellers, take commission\n4. Advertising — free content, earn from ads\n5. Affiliate — earn commission promoting others\n6. Freemium — basic free, premium paid\n7. Licensing — sell rights to use your IP\n8. Services — charge for time/expertise\n\nHARZ uses multiple models:\n- Direct sales (books, ebooks)\n- Marketplace (HarzDM, Getly)\n- Affiliate (10% commission)\n- Services (consultations)\n- Freemium (free AI chat, paid products)"},
    {"instruction": "What is a KPI?", "response": "KPI (Key Performance Indicator) measures how well your business is doing:\n\nCommon KPIs:\n1. Revenue — total money earned\n2. Profit margin — profit as % of revenue\n3. Customer acquisition cost — how much to get one customer\n4. Conversion rate — % of visitors who buy\n5. Average order value — average purchase amount\n6. Customer lifetime value — total a customer spends over time\n7. Churn rate — % of customers who stop buying\n8. ROI — return on investment\n\nHARZ KPIs to track:\n- Books sold per month\n- Revenue (NGN + USD)\n- CRM inquiries and conversion\n- Platform uptime\n- Product upload completion"},
    {"instruction": "What is the sharing economy?", "response": "The sharing economy is people sharing resources for money:\n1. Uber/Bolt — share your car\n2. Airbnb — share your home\n3. TaskRabbit — share your skills/time\n4. Food delivery — share your kitchen or bike\n5. Tool rental — share equipment\n\nPros: extra income, utilize idle resources, flexible\nCons: regulatory issues, no benefits, unstable\n\nIn Nigeria: Bolt and Uber are big. Airbnb is growing. Skill-sharing (freelancing) is the biggest opportunity.\nHARZ enables the sharing economy — sell your knowledge through digital products, share your skills through freelancing."},
    {"instruction": "What is nanotechnology?", "response": "Nanotechnology is engineering at the atomic level:\n1. Working with materials 1-100 nanometers in size\n2. A nanometer is 1 billionth of a meter\n3. Applications: medicine (targeted drug delivery), electronics (smaller chips), materials (stronger, lighter), energy (better batteries)\n4. Future: could revolutionize manufacturing, health, computing\n5. Still mostly in research and early commercial stages\n6. Not directly relevant to most Nigerian businesses yet, but worth knowing about\n\nThe future of technology will be shaped by nanotechnology, AI, and quantum computing."},
    {"instruction": "What is quantum computing?", "response": "Quantum computing uses quantum mechanics for computation:\n1. Regular computers use bits (0 or 1). Quantum uses qubits (both 0 and 1 simultaneously)\n2. Exponentially faster for certain problems\n3. Could solve: drug discovery, climate modeling, cryptography\n4. Not ready for commercial use yet (still experimental)\n5. Companies: Google, IBM, Microsoft investing billions\n6. Could break current encryption (security concern)\n7. 10-20 years from widespread use\nFor now, regular computing is more than enough for any business. But quantum will transform everything when it arrives."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between WiFi and data?", "response": "WiFi vs Mobile Data:\n\nWiFi:\n- Connects to a router (fixed location)\n- Usually faster and more stable\n- Often cheaper (or free at cafes/offices)\n- Limited range\n- Good for: home, office, downloading large files\n\nMobile Data:\n- Connects to cell towers (anywhere with coverage)\n- 3G, 4G, 5G speeds\n- More expensive per GB\n- Works everywhere with network coverage\n- Good for: on-the-go, traveling, areas without WiFi\n\nTips for Nigeria: Use WiFi at home/office for downloads. Switch to data when out. Buy night plans for large downloads (cheaper)."},
    {"instruction": "What is a VPN and should I use one?", "response": "A VPN (Virtual Private Network) encrypts your internet:\n1. Hides your IP address (your location)\n2. Encrypts your data (hackers can't read it)\n3. Bypasses geo-restrictions (access content from other countries)\n4. Protects you on public WiFi\n5. Free VPNs: ProtonVPN, Windscribe (limited data)\n6. Paid VPNs: ExpressVPN, NordVPN (better speed and security)\n\nShould you use one in Nigeria?\n- If you use public WiFi: YES\n- If you want to access international content: YES\n- If you're concerned about privacy: YES\n- For general browsing at home: optional\n\nDon't use free VPNs for banking — they may not be secure enough."},
    {"instruction": "What is a cryptocurrency wallet?", "response": "A crypto wallet stores your digital currencies:\n\nTypes:\n1. Hot wallet — connected to internet (phone apps): Trust Wallet, MetaMask, Phantom\n2. Cold wallet — offline (USB-like device): Ledger, Trezor (most secure)\n3. Exchange wallet — on a crypto exchange: Binance\n\nHow it works:\n1. Wallet has a public address (like account number) — share to receive\n2. Wallet has a private key (like password) — NEVER share\n3. Seed phrase (12-24 words) — backup to restore if phone lost\n\nImportant: If you lose your seed phrase, you lose your crypto forever. Write it on paper, not on your phone.\n\nHARZ uses Polygon wallets for GDEG tokens and TRC20 wallets for USDT."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between a blog and a website?", "response": "Blog vs Website:\n\nBlog:\n- Regularly updated content (articles/posts)\n- Chronological (newest first)\n- Written content focused\n- Personal or business voice\n- Examples: WordPress blog, Medium, Blogger\n\nWebsite:\n- Static or semi-static pages\n- Organized by sections (Home, About, Services, Contact)\n- Can include a blog as one section\n- More structured\n- Examples: Business website, portfolio, e-commerce store\n\nA blog IS a type of website. But not all websites are blogs.\n\nHARZ: Our platform sites are websites. We could add a blog for SEO and content marketing."},
    {"instruction": "What is content marketing?", "response": "Content marketing is creating valuable content to attract customers:\n1. Instead of selling directly, you provide value first\n2. Types: blog posts, videos, podcasts, social media, ebooks\n3. Build trust and authority in your niche\n4. Customers come to you (inbound) instead of you chasing them (outbound)\n5. Long-term strategy (takes 6-12 months to see results)\n6. Very cost-effective (content is free to create)\n7. SEO + content = free traffic for years\n\nHARZ content marketing examples:\n- Free tips on WhatsApp Status\n- Educational posts on social media\n- Our books themselves are content products\n- This AI assistant providing free information\nGive value first, sell second. That's content marketing."},

